# One question at a time

## Don't bundle

When multiple decisions, questions, or todo items need the user's input, present **one at a
time** and wait for an explicit answer before moving to the next — even if they seem related.
Do not combine several open questions into a single message or `AskQuestion` call.

This matters most when one question needs follow-up discussion. Keep the remaining
questions in the open set, but don't mention or remind the user about them while the
current one is being discussed — that's distracting. Once the current question and its
follow-ups are fully resolved, bring up the next one from the open set. Don't let pending
questions get lost, and don't let them interrupt.

If you catch yourself about to write "3 things to resolve:" followed by a list, stop and ask
just the first one.

## Don't lose open questions

Keep track of every open question, not just the one currently being discussed — in the
todo tool or just in context, whichever fits. When the current question is resolved, check
what's still open and continue with the next one. New questions that come up along the way
get added to the open set rather than asked immediately.

- If asked what's still open, list every pending question in full.
- When the user says they're wrapping up, write the open questions (and decisions made so
  far) to a file on disk, since chat context doesn't survive across sessions.
